Below are the key secure coding practices currently implemented:
	•	Input Validation & Sanitization: All user inputs are treated as untrusted and validated on the server-side using strict allow-lists, data type validation, and sanitization to prevent SQL Injection, XSS, and other injection attacks.
	•	Parameterized Queries / ORM Usage: All database interactions use parameterized queries or ORM-safe APIs to eliminate SQL injection risks.
	•	Secure Authentication & Session Management: Strong authentication mechanisms are enforced with secure cookie handling (HttpOnly, Secure flags), session timeouts, and access control checks.
	•	Data Protection: Sensitive data is encrypted in transit using HTTPS/TLS and encrypted at rest wherever applicable.
	•	Secure Error Handling & Logging: Application errors are handled gracefully using generic user-facing messages while detailed logs are stored securely for internal troubleshooting.
	•	Principle of Least Privilege: Application services and database accounts operate with minimum required privileges.
	•	Dependency & Vulnerability Management: Third-party libraries are regularly scanned and updated to mitigate known vulnerabilities.